The user just asked for performance improvements because the current build is laggy and choppy. I’ve already responded with a high‑level plan and started implementing a few of those ideas. The first edit I made reduced the pixel ratio cap to 1.5 and disabled automatic shadow map updates, planning to update shadows only every other frame. That addresses the “adaptive resolution” and “shadow update frequency” parts of the plan.

Now I need to continue applying the other optimization steps. The next biggest performance hit is the very large shadow map (4096×4096) used for the directional sun light. Rendering such a high‑resolution shadow map each frame is expensive, especially when combined with many leaf cards casting shadows. Reducing the shadow map resolution will significantly cut the shadow rendering cost with only a modest visual impact, especially since I’m already limiting the pixel ratio and will be updating shadows less frequently.

I should therefore edit the sun light configuration in *index.html*. I’ll locate the block that sets `sun.castShadow = true;` and the subsequent shadow map size and camera bounds. The current values are:
```
sun.castShadow = true;
sun.shadow.mapSize.set(4096, 4096);
sun.shadow.camera.near = 10;
sun.shadow.camera.far = 360;
sun.shadow.camera.left = -85; sun.shadow.camera.right = 85;
sun.shadow.camera.top = 85; sun.shadow.camera.bottom = -85;
```
To lower the shadow map cost, I’ll halve the resolution to 2048×2048. Additionally, I can shrink the orthographic camera bounds a bit (from ±85 to ±70) because the scene’s visible area doesn’t need the full previous extent; this further reduces the number of shadow map texels covering irrelevant space.

I’ll keep the other parameters (near/far) unchanged because they’re already reasonable. This edit directly implements step 3 of my plan (“shrink the shadow map to 2048”) and also nudges the shadow camera frustum tighter, which should improve shadow quality and performance.

Thus, I’ll issue an Edit tool call targeting the exact old string block and replace it with the new, lower‑resolution configuration.

